Title: The Closing Hour
Project-ID: closing-hour
Logline: On the night before she leaves the city for good, a translator returns a borrowed book to the bookshop owner she once loved, and learns what he kept.
Genre: Romantic drama
Theme: What we keep instead of asking for what we want
Characters: C-001=Nila; C-002=Arjun

INT. PAGE & CANDLE BOOKSHOP - NIGHT #SC-001#

= Nila returns a borrowed paperback minutes after closing; Arjun makes her open it.

[[objective: Nila wants to hand back the book and leave without feeling anything. Arjun wants to get through closing without a scene.]]
[[conflict: Pride and fear. Each is waiting for the other to speak first, and both are hiding behind small talk.]]
[[turning_point: Arjun makes her open the book: inside is a train ticket to her city that he bought and never used.]]
[[outcome: She does not answer his question. She takes the ticket, leaves the book, and turns the sign to OPEN on her way out.]]

Rain ticks on the window. ARJUN counts the till, alone, one lamp lit.

The door chimes. NILA enters, shaking rain from her coat, a worn paperback in her hand.

ARJUN
We're closed.

NILA
I know. I only need a minute.

She sets the paperback on the counter. He doesn't touch it.

NILA
It's yours. I kept it two years. Longer than I meant to.

ARJUN
(too lightly)
You could have mailed it.

NILA
Then I wouldn't have had to see the shop.

A pause. The rain fills it. Nila's eyes drift to a crooked shelf of poetry.

NILA
You never fixed that shelf.

ARJUN
It leans toward the door. Waiting for someone to leave.

Nila laughs, small, then stops herself. She turns to go.

ARJUN
Nila. Open it.

She stops. He nods at the book. She opens it. Inside: a train ticket to her city, two years old, unused.

NILA
You bought this.

ARJUN
The day after you left. I stood on the platform until the train pulled away.

NILA
Why tell me now?

ARJUN
Because you leave at dawn, and I'm still counting the till.

Nila folds the ticket along its old crease. Her hand trembles. His does too.

NILA
Ask me.

ARJUN
Stay.

She holds his gaze. Doesn't answer. Pockets the ticket, leaves the book on the counter, and walks to the door.

At the threshold she stops, turns the CLOSED sign to OPEN, and steps out into the rain, leaving the door ajar.
